From: faliye <faliye@example.com>
Subject: [PATCH] thunderbolt: net: do not enable E2E flow control on the Tx ring

Commit "net: thunderbolt: Enable e2e flow control in two direction" moved
the RING_FLAG_E2E setup so that the flag is applied to the Tx ring as well
as the Rx ring.

Per the USB4 spec, when E2E flow control is *disabled* for a Transmit
Descriptor Ring the Host Interface Adapter Layer does not require any
credits to be available before transmitting a tunneled packet from that
ring. Conversely, once E2E is *enabled* on the Tx ring the controller must
obtain end-to-end credits before it may transmit.

On the ASMedia ASM4242 USB4/TB3 host router (PCI id 1b21:2425) those
credits are never delivered. The controller accepts the configuration --
the ring OPTIONS register reads back exactly what was written, including
RING_FLAG_E2E_FLOW_CONTROL (bit 28) and the E2E HopID field -- but no
credit ever arrives, so the Tx ring's hardware consumer index never
advances. The link still passes small/intermittent traffic (ICMP squeaks
through) while any sustained transfer stops dead.

Symptoms on this controller with the offending commit applied:

  - bulk TCP/UDP throughput drops to 0; iperf3 reports "No route to host"
    once the neighbour entry expires, while ping still succeeds
  - the driver's stall-detection counters climb without bound (a
    frozen-consumer watchdog fired ~30k times in a 10 s window)
  - latency-sensitive request/response traffic sees a fraction of round
    trips stall for the peer's RTO, because the parked descriptor is only
    released once some later event pokes the ring

Restricting RING_FLAG_E2E to the Rx ring restores the pre-commit behaviour
and makes the link work again. Measured on two hosts joined point to point
(MSI X870E/X870, onboard ASM4242, kernel 6.17):

  Tx ring E2E enabled : 0 Gb/s, watchdog storm, link unusable
  Tx ring E2E disabled: 5.13 Gb/s steady, 0 TCP retransmits, 0 stalls in
                        9000 x 16 KB request/response round trips

Rx-side E2E is kept, so hosts that do implement the credit return still
get receive-side flow control.

A follow-up could re-enable Tx-side E2E behind a controller quirk once it
is confirmed which silicon actually implements the credit return; blanket
enabling it regresses at least this controller.

Fixes: ("net: thunderbolt: Enable e2e flow control in two direction")
Signed-off-by: faliye <faliye@example.com>
Cc: Andreas Noever <andreas.noever@gmail.com>
Cc: Mika Westerberg <westeri@kernel.org>
Cc: Yehezkel Bernat <YehezkelShB@gmail.com>
Cc: linux-usb@vger.kernel.org
Cc: netdev@vger.kernel.org
---
 drivers/net/thunderbolt/main.c | 9 ++++++---
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/thunderbolt/main.c b/drivers/net/thunderbolt/main.c
--- a/drivers/net/thunderbolt/main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/thunderbolt/main.c
@@ -929,15 +929,18 @@ static int tbnet_open(struct net_device *dev)
 	struct tb_xdomain *xd = net->xd;
 	u16 sof_mask, eof_mask;
 	struct tb_ring *ring;
-	unsigned int flags;
+	unsigned int flags, rx_flags;
 	int hopid;

 	netif_carrier_off(dev);

 	flags = RING_FLAG_FRAME;
-	/* Only enable full E2E if the other end supports it too */
+	rx_flags = flags;
+	/*
+	 * Only enable full E2E if the other end supports it too. Keep it off
+	 * the Tx ring: with E2E enabled there the controller must obtain
+	 * end-to-end credits before it may transmit, and not every host
+	 * router actually delivers them (ASMedia ASM4242 never does, which
+	 * parks the Tx consumer index forever).
+	 */
 	if (tbnet_e2e && net->svc->prtcstns & TBNET_E2E)
-		flags |= RING_FLAG_E2E;
+		rx_flags |= RING_FLAG_E2E;

 	ring = tb_ring_alloc_tx(xd->tb->nhi, -1, TBNET_RING_SIZE, flags);
 	if (!ring) {
@@ -952,7 +955,7 @@ static int tbnet_open(struct net_device *dev)
 	sof_mask = BIT(TBIP_PDF_FRAME_START);
 	eof_mask = BIT(TBIP_PDF_FRAME_END);

-	ring = tb_ring_alloc_rx(xd->tb->nhi, -1, TBNET_RING_SIZE, flags,
+	ring = tb_ring_alloc_rx(xd->tb->nhi, -1, TBNET_RING_SIZE, rx_flags,
 				net->tx_ring.ring->hop, sof_mask,
 				eof_mask, tbnet_start_poll, net);
 	if (!ring) {
